// lavition_ui — the editor's side panel: layout, hit-test and text metrics.
//
// lavition's, not Moros's. It holds no world, no lattice and no tool: a
// consumer hands it a `PanelSpec` of labels and indices, and gets back
// rectangles and hits. See doc/claude/EDITOR_UI.md.
// ⚠ `self::`, ON EVERY ONE — loft#976, and this package is the reason it matters.
// A module's BASENAME is global across a consumer's whole dependency graph, so a
// bare `use render;` here claims the name `render` in every graph this library is
// pulled into — and the registry's `graphics` has shipped a `src/render.loft` since
// 0.1.0. Two bare claims and the CONSUMER's `use` line order decides which library
// loses its module, reported as an unknown function in a file its author never
// touched. `src/editor_client.loft` already links both.
//
// ⚠ NOTHING IS RED TODAY and that is not the test. `graphics` does not claim its
// `render` bare, so `tools/basenames.sh` passes — the rule it enforces is *two bare
// claims*, not *two files*. What changes on publication is that this package becomes
// a stranger in graphs nobody here can see, where the other claim is somebody else's
// to make. `panel`, `font`, `widgets` and `render` are four of the most ordinary
// names a UI library could hold.
use self::widgets;
use self::font;
use self::panel;
use self::render;
use self::verbbar;
